  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "Given that the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{24{x}^{3}-1}{2c{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}}$ is $y=-\\frac{1}{3},$ find the value of $c.$",
  "worked_solution": "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{3}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{24{x}^{3}-1}{2c{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{24{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{2c{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{15{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(24-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(2c-\\frac{15}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{24-0}{2c-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{c}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{12}{c} & =-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ c & =-36.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
